layout "layouts/section.faqir"

block lede
  p Templates compose two ways: a page fills a layout's blocks, and a partial is included with parameters. Registry components are called by name and checked against their manifests. Everything the server draws is markup faqir-core already knows, so the browser takes it over.

block toc
  li
    a(href="#composition") Layouts, blocks and includes
  li
    a(href="#components") Component calls
  li
    a(href="#island") The l-data island
  li
    a(href="#hydration") Server-rendered recipes in the browser

block content
  h2#composition Layouts, blocks and includes
  p
    | A file whose first statement is
    code layout
    | is a page, and the rest of it is
    code block
    | definitions. In the layout, each block marks a place and its children are the default. The page's block replaces those children and is rendered with the page's data.
  p
    | A layout does not have to be a whole document. The one below draws a card, and puts two of its blocks inside the card's slots. The page fills the title and the content, and leaves the footer alone, so the layout's default footer is what you see.
  include "partials/example.faqir" (example=ex.layout)
  p
    | There is one layout level, blocks only replace, and a block the layout does not have is a compile error rather than content that silently disappears.
  include "partials/failure.faqir" (failure=err.block)

  h3#composition-include Includes with parameters
  p
    | A partial is the one reusable piece of markup. It is rendered with a fresh scope that holds only the parameters the
    code include
    | line passes, so it reads the same wherever it is included. Parameters are written like attributes: an expression, a quoted literal with holes, or a bare name meaning true.
  include "partials/example.faqir" (example=ex.include)
  p
    | Notice
    code aria-current
    | on one link only. The comparison
    code item.href == current
    | is made per row by the caller and arrives in the partial as the boolean
    code current
    | which the partial turns into the attribute or leaves out.
  p
    | The partial's first line,
    code params href label current=false
    | declares what it takes. A bare name must be passed and a name with a value has a default, so the partial reads
    code current
    | without a fallback, and an include that passes a parameter the partial does not declare, or leaves out one it needs, is a compile error.
  p
    | Nothing else crosses into the partial. The page below has a user in its data, and the partial still cannot see it.
  include "partials/failure.faqir" (failure=err.scope)
  p
    | Names are relative to the loader's root from every file, never to the including file. An include chain that returns to a file already on it, or that nests deeper than 16, is found when the template compiles. A partial recurses only through an include that carries a depth budget, such as depth=3, which the render enforces.

  h2#components Component calls
  p
    | A line starting with
    code +
    | calls a registry component: the engine writes that component's reference markup from its manifest, with the same attributes, ARIA wiring and hidden guards the registry ships. The table this site compiles with describes {{ facts.callable }} callable components. Each argument is matched against the manifest, in this order:
  div(data-docs-table tabindex="0")
    table
      thead
        tr
          th Argument
          th Written as
          th Checked
      tbody
        tr
          td
            | A variant, named by its attribute without
            code data-
            | such as
            code variant
            | or
            code size
          td The manifest's attribute, on the element the manifest puts it on. The default writes nothing.
          td A literal at compile time, an expression at render time, against the manifest's values
        tr
          td
            | A state, such as
            code loading
            | or
            code disabled
          td The manifest's attribute on the root, when the value is truthy
          td Bare, or an expression tested for truthiness
        tr
          td
            | A string prop, such as
            code title
          td The text or attribute the reference markup uses it for
          td A literal or an expression giving text
        tr
          td
            code heading
            | on a component whose markup has headings, such as a card's title
          td The level of those headings, so a card under a page title can be an h2
          td A literal h1 to h6
        tr
          td
            code id
          td The component's id, which every id inside it derives from
          td Unset, the render's next id
        tr
          td
            | Attributes such as
            code role
            | and
            code aria-*
            | and any faqir-core attribute
          td Passed through onto the root
          td As on a plain element
  p
    | Named slots are filled with
    code @slot
    | lines under the call; a component whose manifest says its content is inline, text or block also takes plain children as its default slot. Here a card takes its title and description as props, its body and footer as fills, and nests a badge and a button inside.
  include "partials/example.faqir" (example=ex.call)
  p
    | Notice what the manifests decided. The badge's variant came from the data and was checked against the badge's allowed values when the page rendered. The button's size became
    code data-size
    | while its default variant wrote nothing at all.
  p
    | States are booleans. A state that is on writes the manifest's attribute; a state that is off writes nothing.
  include "partials/example.faqir" (example=ex.states)
  p
    | A wrong argument is a compile error that lists what the component does take, and the five protocol attributes cannot be written by hand on a call, because the manifest's own names are the only way to reach them.
  include "partials/failure.faqir" (failure=err.argument)
  include "partials/failure.faqir" (failure=err.protocol)
  p
    | A variant that comes from data cannot be checked when the template compiles, so it is checked when it renders, against the same list.
  include "partials/failure.faqir" (failure=err.variant)
  h3#components-plain Plain elements
  p
    | A plain element may carry
    code data-ui
    | too. It is written as you typed it and not checked as a call. That is how you write a component that is also a part of another one, such as a button that is a dialog's trigger, because a call never takes
    code data-part
  h3#components-repeat Repeating parts
  p
    | A slot filled more than once, or filled inside
    code each
    | or
    code if
    | under the call, repeats its part: one breadcrumb link, step, tab or accordion item per fill, in the order written. A fill whose lines are fills of its own fills the parts inside it, and a fill takes arguments in parentheses: the states the manifest gives that part, and attributes such as an href. Optional parts left unfilled are not written at all.
  include "partials/example.faqir" (example=ex.repeat)
  p Notice that each breadcrumb link came with its list item and the separator after it, which is what repeats with a link in the breadcrumb's reference markup, and that each step took its state from the data. Ids inside a repeated part are numbered per copy, so the tabs below pair trigger N with panel N however many the data holds.

  h2#island The l-data island
  p
    | Server data reaches faqir-core's evaluator in exactly one way:
    code l-data=json(expr)
    | which writes the value as a JSON literal, escaped for the attribute. JSON text is a pure literal to the client, so a string in the data cannot become code. The expressions the client runs, such as
    code count++
    | are literal strings the template's author wrote, and they pass through untouched.
  include "partials/example.faqir" (example=ex.island)
  p
    | Notice that the count already reads 2 when the page arrives: the server drew it as text, and
    code l-text
    | keeps it in step once the island is live. Press Add one to see it. The note in the data, with its quotes and its closing tag, sits inside the attribute as escaped JSON and never reaches the page as markup.
  p
    | Data in any other faqir-core attribute is refused when the template compiles, and so is
    code json()
    | anywhere but the whole value of
    code l-data
  include "partials/failure.faqir" (failure=err.interpolation)
  include "partials/failure.faqir" (failure=err.misplaced)

  h2#hydration Server-rendered recipes in the browser
  p
    | The three recipes below were drawn by the engine from data when this site was built. Nothing in the browser knows that. faqir-core finds
    code data-ui="tabs"
    | and its siblings, runs their controllers, and from then on they behave exactly as recipes written by hand. Each result starts with an
    code @ui:requires
    | comment naming the engine and the controllers it needs, and this page loads faqir-core because of it.
  h3#hydration-tabs Tabs
  include "partials/example.faqir" (example=ex.tabs)
  p
    | Click a tab, or focus the selected tab and press
    +kbd ←
    | or
    +kbd →
    | to move between them.
  h3#hydration-accordion Accordion
  include "partials/example.faqir" (example=ex.accordion)
  p
    | Click a question, or focus it and press
    +kbd Enter
    | to open it. Opening another closes the first.
  h3#hydration-dialog Dialog
  include "partials/example.faqir" (example=ex.dialog)
  p
    | Open the dialog, then press
    +kbd Esc
    | to close it. Focus returns to the button that opened it.
  p
    | A dialog is an overlay, and an overlay inside the page's main content breaks the audit's landmark rule. So the dialog comes from a second template,
    code dialog.overlay.faqir
    | rendered with the same data and mounted beside the main content. The button reaches it through the controller's
    code data-open
    | hook, which a call passes through like any other attribute. The Cancel button is a plain element with
    code data-ui="button"
    | because it is a part of the dialog as well as a button, and so is Delete, which in this demo only closes it.
